# {{.Name}}

A TypeScript wefty workflow scaffolded by `wefty workflow init {{.Name}} --lang ts`.
It greets a subject, reports one step, one envelope and one gate, and hands
back a result document. Replace the work; keep the reporting shape.

## What this workflow may do

It reports, and it uses no credential to do it. Reporting goes through the
**run mailbox** — files written into `$WEFTY_RUN_DIR`, which the node agent
publishes to the run ledger with the credential it already holds. A mailbox
write is a claim about this run and nothing else: it confers no authority over
the cluster, so do not build on this workflow being able to dispatch a child
run, read another run, or act on any other job.

The protocol is `docs/contracts/run-execution-context.md`, "Run mailbox". The
mailbox is delivered to every one-shot L3 dispatched, `kind=process` and
`kind=oci` alike.

## Node version

**Node.js 22.7 or later**, both where you build and on the node that runs the
workflow. The bundle targets `node22`, and the node runs it as a file with no
extension: Node reads that file as an ES module only because of module syntax
detection, which is on by default from 22.7. An older Node fails with
`SyntaxError: Cannot use import statement outside a module`.

## Files

| File | What it is |
|---|---|
| `src/{{.Name}}.ts` | the workflow, including an inline run-mailbox writer |
| `package.json` | the build: esbuild bundles `src/{{.Name}}.ts` to `dist/{{.Name}}.mjs` |
| `package-lock.json` | the pinned toolchain (esbuild, TypeScript, Node types) |
| `tsconfig.json` | strict type checking; `npm run typecheck` |
| `params.json` | the params the submit command below sends |
| `{{.TestFile}}` | runs the built bundle the way a node does and asserts the four events |

The workflow depends on no package at run time. The writer at the top of
`src/{{.Name}}.ts` writes the same event bytes as `wefty run`, which wefty's
conformance tests hold, so the workflow reports the same way whether or not
the image it runs in ships the `wefty` binary. It refuses what the agent would
refuse, creates each event file exclusively with mode 0600 and flushes it
before the rename. The one thing it cannot do that `wefty run` does is resolve
every path under a single opened directory, so a directory swapped inside the
mailbox between its check and its write is not caught; the exact limits are
documented above the writer.

One ordering limit applies to every producer: the agent publishes a sweep in
the lexical order of the event file names, and a name carries a nanosecond
stamp plus a per-process sequence. Node's clock reads milliseconds, so this
writer forces its own stamps to increase. Sequential reporting from this
process is ordered; two concurrent writers are not.

## Build

The build step is yours: a submission carries one inline script, so the
workflow is bundled into one self-contained file before it is submitted.

```sh
npm ci
npm run build        # dist/{{.Name}}.mjs
npm test             # typecheck, build, then go test (below)
```

Rebuild after every change to `src/`: the cluster runs the bundle, not the
source.

## Run it

Submit the built bundle, follow it, then read what it reported:

```sh
wefty --json submit \
  --script=dist/{{.Name}}.mjs \
  --interpreter=node \
  --params-file=params.json \
  --tag=<routing-tag> \
  --required-envelope \
  --idempotency-key=<stable-unique-key>

wefty logs <run_id> --follow      # wait for it, live
wefty --json inspect <run_id>     # the envelopes, gates and lineage it produced
wefty results <run_id>            # the result document
```

`--interpreter=node` is resolved on the node's `PATH`, so the node needs Node.js
22.7 or later installed. `--required-envelope` is not optional advice: without
it a job that exits 0 having reported nothing still looks like a success, which
is the one failure this whole reporting shape exists to prevent.

`params.json` is an ordinary JSON object, scaffolded next to this README; this
starter reads `subject`:

```json
{"subject": "wefty"}
```

A job never receives its own params in the environment. The agent delivers them
to `$WEFTY_RUN_DIR/params.json`, which is what `runParam("subject")` reads.

## Local exercise

The workflow does not need a cluster to be exercised: point `WEFTY_RUN_DIR` at
an empty directory, run the bundle, then read `events/`.

```sh
npm run build
export WEFTY_RUN_DIR=$(mktemp -d)
export WEFTY_HANDOFF_DIR=$(mktemp -d)
node dist/{{.Name}}.mjs
ls "$WEFTY_RUN_DIR/events"
```

`{{.TestFile}}` does exactly that, from a copy of the bundle written without an
extension the way the node writes it. `npm test` runs it with `go test`, which
needs the workflow to sit inside a Go module — in the wefty repository it
does.
